 and bass scenes what inspires you to create such interesting sounds it's always been like my thing to kind of blend things together yeah like uh right now the the the more time goes by and the more uh people can reach to new music from everywhere in the world yes that's true uh but a while back it wasn't that easy to cross over stuff so there was like the uk scene the us scene the every everyone has kind of his own thing the french dutch all that but now everything is freely shared and spread and uh i feel it's just like what i've been doing since the beginning trying to to blend everything together uh was global is is the name now but back then it was just like whatever you wanted to call it yeah that's cool um and from that how did you kind of like blend your music from djing you know because you said earlier you started doing hip-hop you know so do you have elements of hip-hop that you bring within you know your sound and you know do you bring other elements from like your french touch you know like well before i left london my i was into uh a rock yeah i was playing the drum all that yes yeah and the hip-hop from my other cousins that were into this yeah really into the u.s vibe uh and then uh the club scene like came to me and it was more of a electro scene you know all the french the french vibe even even in london was that one of the biggest uh we took it like bloody roots or all the institute the headbanger guys all that uh so the first thing i did was try to mix the hip-hop with the electro so it was hip-hop beats and electro synths on that and uh it came out to be a thing uh it was called trap yeah a few years later uh so that's what i've been doing for a while uh when i was in london yes started the trap label uh yeah and then i saw no one was doing it in paris so i went back and started doing the nights and the music and everything under the name fletcher yeah oh wow it was a crossover between like trap and arabic global sounds yeah yeah and middle eastern so it went well and i love the way people were surprised about the drops in that style it's like you've got the build-up that you get in any electro songs which is like kind of usual for them and then you've got that drop that is uh really you gotta think a few seconds before you start enjoying it and seeing like you gotta think and then move which is what i like to see people doing is just like try to think yeah when they are on the dance floor nice uh and surprise them so yeah surprise but at some point it wasn't that surprising anymore because it was like getting really big yeah uh that's where i decided to make the switch to find something else unusual so i came to buy the phone because the first time you heard it i when i heard it the first time i was like wow he's this yeah yeah i wasn't even like that's good i was like that's okay that's surprising might be what i need now so i switched and uh no it's download yeah beautiful yeah i think when we first started talking years ago now it was your production that really stood out and also just how easy you make it for people to connect with you know so like music is very like not too complex but you know it's like fun you know yeah that was the original point just to take whatever the Brazilian were doing which was crazy and that is something that I cannot do because I just don't have vibe like they just with two sounds they will make a yeah amazing song that is too groovy and all that and i don't have this it's like a cultural thing yeah but uh what i had was the past the electronic and the knowledge of like mixing and mastering all that so i was like let's make it so people in europe and yeah can can listen to it and not like go out of the club uh like they will do if I just play a role by Le Fanc from Brazil. So that was the original idea, just to make it so people can enjoy it with their European or Occidental ears. Definitely. Thank you so much, Don. We're going to get you to get on the DJ decks and play a mix. Would you like to tell the listeners a bit about what your sound is currently? Right now, I've always liked heavy stuff. especially coming from the London music scene with the dubstep, the drum bass, all that. So I've always been kind of heavy on what I do. But I still like a bit of groove sometimes, so I try to go around this from heavy to groovy without being too awkward. Good stuff. You're listening to me, Hente, and my people on FMI Radio 94.5, And we got special guest Dunler about to select a guest mix."}, {"start": "00:59:10", "end": "01:00:00", "text": "And that was Don Lowe, all the way from Spain via France, guest selector for today's show. Thank you so much. Yeah, thank you. Thank you for welcoming me here. Such a high, upbeat energy with all the tracks you play. Definitely heavy, pesado as they say. Yeah, yeah. Beautiful. Don Lowe, where can the people find you online? everything pretty much is going to be with the handle I am download I am download so I am download D-O-N-L-O-W I am beautiful and where to next what's next after your Australian tour yeah going to get back in the studio yeah beautiful yeah easy thank you again for joining us here at FBI Radio up next we have Tyson Kerr with Loose Joints keep it locked"}], "fbi_json": [{"timestamp": {"enabled": true, "formatted": "00:00:33"}, "track": {"title": "Nohemí", "artists": [{"name": "Andrés Landero"}]}, "isPremiere": false, "_confidence": "low"}, {"timestamp": {"enabled": true, "formatted": "00:00:47"}, "track": {"title": "La cumbia me está llamando (feat. La Perla) [Kaidi Tatham Remix]", "artists": [{"name": "Nubya Garcia"}]}, "isPremiere": false, "_confidence": "mid"}, {"timestamp": {"enabled": true, "formatted": "00:06:08"}, "track": {"title": "Llorando Se Fue", "artists": [{"name": "Grupo Tropicalisimo"}]}, "isPremiere": false, "_confidence": "mid"}, {"timestamp": {"enabled": true, "formatted": "00:10:00"}, "track": {"title": "C.R.E.A.M. (feat. Method Man, Raekwon, Inspectah Deck & Buddha